  "notes": "West's club lead is won by South. South's club loser can be ruffed in the dummy leaving the heart suit as the only possible problem. At first glance, it appears that the contract will depend on the heart finesse.\n\nSouth can sometimes make the contract when West has the ♥K by playing for an elimination. South should start by drawing 2 rounds of trumps in the closed hand. When diamonds prove to be 2-2 half of the battle is over.\n\nThe ♠A and ♠K are cashed and a third round of spades is ruffed. South should next cash the other top club honour and ruff the ♣2 in dummy.\n\nDummy's last spade is played. If East follows, South will need the heart finesse to work. When East shows out on the fourth round of spades the contract is certain. South discards the ♥2 allowing West to win the trick. This play is called a loser-on-loser play.\n\nWest is endplayed. A heart return will eliminate South's heart loser regardless of the position of the ♥K. A club return will concede a ruff and discard.",
